{"exhaustive":{"nbHits":false,"typo":false},"exhaustiveNbHits":false,"exhaustiveTypo":false,"hits":[{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"jonhohle"},"comment_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["rate","limit","agent"],"value":"Most people <em>aren\u2019t</em> familiar with a peak demand <em>rate</em> because it\u2019s not common in many markets. Take the highest kW use period of the month, and multiply kW demand by $20. Run the oven; pulls 3kW; $60. Run the oven and the stove top (1.8kW) at the same time $96. Screw up one time during the month and you might as well not care about the rest of the month.<p>I\u2019m being a bit hyperbolic - it\u2019s averaged over the highest 1 hour peak demand period (4pm-7pm, M-F), but it does <em>limit</em> what things can be run simultaneously (no dryer, oven and AC at the same time, for example). We schedule things around that, Our peak demand for this past month was 2.3kW. That single line item charge is higher than our entire gas bill.<p>There\u2019s a separate use charge as well, but per kWh that\u2019s pretty low. Then, of course, there\u2019s the dozens of other random charges and fees ($86) that have nothing to do with electricity generation and everything to do with being a monopoly (court resolution surcharge: $3.58). The idea of moving more things to electric is nauseating."},"story_title":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"Electrification Could Cut Global Energy Use in Half"},"story_url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"https://fullycharged.show/podcasts/podcast-361-why-electrification-could-cut-global-energy-use-in-half/"}},"_tags":["comment","author_jonhohle","story_50319110"],"author":"jonhohle","children":[50327093,50328721,50326375,50326646],"comment_text":"Most people aren\u2019t familiar with a peak demand rate because it\u2019s not common in many markets. Take the highest kW use period of the month, and multiply kW demand by $20. Run the oven; pulls 3kW; $60. Run the oven and the stove top (1.8kW) at the same time $96. Screw up one time during the month and you might as well not care about the rest of the month.<p>I\u2019m being a bit hyperbolic - it\u2019s averaged over the highest 1 hour peak demand period (4pm-7pm, M-F), but it does limit what things can be run simultaneously (no dryer, oven and AC at the same time, for example). We schedule things around that, Our peak demand for this past month was 2.3kW. That single line item charge is higher than our entire gas bill.<p>There\u2019s a separate use charge as well, but per kWh that\u2019s pretty low. Then, of course, there\u2019s the dozens of other random charges and fees ($86) that have nothing to do with electricity generation and everything to do with being a monopoly (court resolution surcharge: $3.58). The idea of moving more things to electric is nauseating.","created_at":"2026-11-01T15:00:53Z","created_at_i":1793545253,"objectID":"50326315","parent_id":50325921,"story_id":50319110,"story_title":"Electrification Could Cut Global Energy Use in Half","story_url":"https://fullycharged.show/podcasts/podcast-361-why-electrification-could-cut-global-energy-use-in-half/","updated_at":"2026-11-02T04:38:12Z"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"thaumasiotes"},"comment_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["rate","limit","agent"],"value":"No, the definition of 'stealth bot' is a disaster. The proposed text criminalizes altering your user-<em>agent</em> string. It arguably criminalizes all existing user-<em>agent</em> strings, since their whole purpose is to lie about which browser you are.<p>Taking the first thing I could find, geekflare.com informs me that the current user-<em>agent</em> string for Google Chrome running on Windows 11 is<p><pre><code>    Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 10.0; Win64; x64) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/155.0.0.0 Safari/537.36\n</code></pre>\nWhat's doing the work in the proposed text is the prohibition on using software &quot;in a manner that is reasonably likely to damage, impair, or burden the technical or commercial operation of&quot; a service.<p>&gt; Someone who is using a residential proxy network, headless browsing, and/or TLS fingerprint manipulation to evade <em>rate</em> <em>limits</em> and other controls to issue large volumes of automated requests under a deceptive UserAgent string isn't difficult to describe and differentiate versus a normal user using a UA.<p>Note that someone who's scripting a headless browser doesn't need to use a deceptive user-<em>agent</em> string. They can use the user-<em>agent</em> string for their browser, which isn't deceptive except to the degree that all user-<em>agent</em> strings are 90+% lies."},"story_title":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"Breaking the open web won't stop the bad bots"},"story_url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"https://www.techpolicy.press/breaking-the-open-web-wont-stop-the-bad-bots/"}},"_tags":["comment","author_thaumasiotes","story_50263837"],"author":"thaumasiotes","children":[50314987],"comment_text":"No, the definition of &#x27;stealth bot&#x27; is a disaster. The proposed text criminalizes altering your user-agent string. It arguably criminalizes all existing user-agent strings, since their whole purpose is to lie about which browser you are.<p>Taking the first thing I could find, geekflare.com informs me that the current user-agent string for Google Chrome running on Windows 11 is<p><pre><code>    Mozilla&#x2F;5.0 (Windows NT 10.0; Win64; x64) AppleWebKit&#x2F;537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome&#x2F;155.0.0.0 Safari&#x2F;537.36\n</code></pre>\nWhat&#x27;s doing the work in the proposed text is the prohibition on using software &quot;in a manner that is reasonably likely to damage, impair, or burden the technical or commercial operation of&quot; a service.<p>&gt; Someone who is using a residential proxy network, headless browsing, and&#x2F;or TLS fingerprint manipulation to evade rate limits and other controls to issue large volumes of automated requests under a deceptive UserAgent string isn&#x27;t difficult to describe and differentiate versus a normal user using a UA.<p>Note that someone who&#x27;s scripting a headless browser doesn&#x27;t need to use a deceptive user-agent string. They can use the user-agent string for their browser, which isn&#x27;t deceptive except to the degree that all user-agent strings are 90+% lies.","created_at":"2026-10-31T10:12:25Z","created_at_i":1793441545,"objectID":"50314610","parent_id":50309766,"story_id":50263837,"story_title":"Breaking the open web won't stop the bad bots","story_url":"https://www.techpolicy.press/breaking-the-open-web-wont-stop-the-bad-bots/","updated_at":"2026-10-31T18:57:21Z"}],"hitsPerPage":2,"nbHits":22,"nbPages":11,"page":0,"params":"query=rate+limit+agent&tags=%28story%2Ccomment%29&hitsPerPage=2&page=0&numericFilters=created_at_i%3E%3D1791004054%2Ccreated_at_i%3C%3D1793596054&advancedSyntax=true&analyticsTags=backend","processingTimeMS":8,"processingTimingsMS":{"_request":{"queue":12,"roundTrip":18},"fetch":{"query":6,"total":7},"total":8},"query":"rate limit agent","serverTimeMS":20}
